   "title": "the owner's agy-reserved-for-measurement ruling lives in the carrier as PROSE and panel.sh knows nothing about it -- so agy was fired in the very next panel",
   "witness": "harness:panel_20260830_214609/ -- agy.txt exists in a routine panel fired AFTER the ruling; `grep -ci 'reserved' panel.sh` returns 0, and the launch at panel.sh's AGY block is unconditional.",
   "note": "*** THIS IS THE CARRIER'S OWN STRUCTURE-FIRST FAILURE, MEASURED AGAINST ITSELF WITHIN TWO HOURS OF THE RULING BEING RECORDED. ***  The owner ruled 2026-08-30 that **agy is RESERVED FOR MEASUREMENT and held out of routine panels**, on the warrant that it is the only compile-and-measure seat NOT behind the Agent-tool transport -- the roster's insurance against the outage that once cost a round all its measuring capacity at once.  **The ruling was written into the carrier.  `panel.sh` was not touched.**  `grep -ci 'reserved for measurement' panel.sh` returns **0**, its AGY block is unconditional, and `panel_20260830_214609/` -- a routine panel fired after the ruling -- contains an `agy.txt`.  *The carrier's own diagnosis applies verbatim: knowing a rule and having it fire on the right object are different problems, and only the second is solved by writing more.  A rule that earns its second instance while sitting in that file has thereby proven it was mis-filed as prose.  This one earned its FIRST instance in under two hours.*  **THE FIX FOLLOWS THE `OLLAMA4` PRECEDENT EXACTLY, AND THE PRECEDENT'S WARNING IS THE LOAD-BEARING PART:** a variable, plus a conditional BANNER, LAUNCH, SEAT CHECK and VERDICT ROSTER.  The carrier records why all four: *blanking the variable alone would have left `panel_ollama.py` running with an empty model name, writing an error file, and producing a PHANTOM SEAT that answers the seat check.*  The agy shape is different but the hazard is identical -- a blank binary path would fail loudly rather than phantom, but the SEAT CHECK would then report a seat FAILURE where the truth is a deliberate omission.  *** AND THAT DISTINCTION IS THE WHOLE POINT OF THE RULING'S COST CLAUSE: a seat DELIBERATELY NOT FIRED is a different thing from a seat FAILURE and a different thing again from AGREEMENT.  Conflating them is what the seat ledger exists to refuse. ***  So the seat check must print DELIBERATELY NOT FIRED by name, every panel, rather than silently omitting agy or reporting it down.  **DEFERRED, NOT FORGOTTEN, AND THE REASON IS A RULE THIS PROJECT HAS ALREADY PAID FOR:** a panel was RUNNING when this was found, and **bash reads a script incrementally by byte offset**, so editing `panel.sh` mid-run corrupts that run -- the failure signature being a syntax error in a file that parses clean afterwards.  The edit waits for the panel to finish.  *The urge to edit arrives exactly when you are watching the script run, which is why the rule exists.*"
  },
