   "title": "sgi_re_width_probe.py's `other kill` row is a DEATH-ONLY oracle -- a one-character change to the text it claims to pin scores 35/35, MEASURED",
   "witness": "harness:MEASURED by the #497 pass-2 seat in its own clone, with the prediction filed BEFORE building.  base 35/35 CONTROL=OK; x_othertext (catch-all message text altered, exit(1) untouched) 35/35 with ZERO red rows; x_readtext (one read arm's text altered) 34/35, reddening `tlb_a ld kill got=exit1-txt want=exit1+txt`.  The matched pair is the point: it proves the six siblings ARE failable and this one is not.",
   "note": "*** THE ROUND APPLIED THE DEATH-AND-TEXT DISCIPLINE TO SIX ROWS AND MISSED THE SEVENTH. ***  `sgi_re_width_probe.py`'s catch-all row captures the transcript and **throws it away** -- `row(\"other kill\", how, \"exit1\", ...)` uses only the how-it-died value -- while its six `ld kill` siblings twelve lines above compute an `ok_txt` membership test and report `exit1+txt`.  **So the row's own note claims IDENTITY -- *the catch-all is untouched* -- on an oracle that observes only DEATH.**  Any edit that keeps the arm fatal while changing what it is passes.  *** IT IS THE `d_returnzero` LESSON MIRRORED ONTO THE DEATH SIDE. ***  That one was a survival-only oracle at a site where SURVIVAL is the shared answer; this is a death-only oracle at a site where DEATH is the shared answer.  Same shape, opposite polarity, and the round fixed the first while shipping the second.  **AND FINDING IT DISCHARGED AN OBLIGATION THE TWO SHIPPED TABLES NEVER DID.**  Across all 41 preserved probe outputs, **SEVEN rows were red under NO mutant in either table** -- the six `ld kill` and `other kill`.  `PRETABLE.out` names their channel as *a round that silently removed the READ kills*, **but no such mutant was ever built**, so for those seven the CHANNEL line was satisfied ON PAPER.  The pass-2 pair now measures six of the seven failable.  **One remains measurably blind, and it is this row.**  **DISPOSITION: COVERAGE DEBT, NOT A FLOOR BREACH -- and the reasoning is the seat's, recorded so nobody re-argues it.**  Clauses (a), (b) and (c) pass, and the row DOES red against a null medium.  The catch-all is not a predicate the fix DEPENDS ON, so the bounded (d) closure does not reach it.  And clause (e)'s contract says *silent REMOVAL*, which this mutant is not -- **the contract is precise even though the row's inline note overclaims.**  **THE FIX IS ONE LINE:** make `other kill` symmetric with its six siblings by adding the text membership test.  It is deliberately NOT done in-round -- editing a shipped detector row means re-measuring, and the round's stopping rule fixes only measured FALSE PASSES and WRONG RECORDS.  *This is neither: it is a row that is weaker than its note claims, which the note has now been corrected to say.*  *** RECORDS DEBT, NAMED BY THE fable5 SEAT AND CONFIRMED BY LOOKING: THE 35/35 AND 34/35 RUNS ARE CITED IN TWO COMMIT MESSAGES, THIS ROW, THE BUG FILE AND A PROBE COMMENT -- AND THEIR LOGS ARE NOT PRESERVED ANYWHERE DURABLE. ***  `34/35` appears under `regress/artifacts/widthkillclass/opus_impl_20260830/` only in `PREDICTIONS.out` and `PRETABLE.out`, which are PREDICTIONS written BEFORE the run -- not the measurement.  The measuring seat built `x_othertext` and `x_readtext` in its own scratch directory and reported the scores; **the Agent seats leave no file in the panel dir**, so even the report is in volatile temp.  **This is the volatile-path rule breached from a direction it does not cover: the rule says copy the log before the record cites it, and here the record cites a log THAT WAS NEVER MINE TO COPY.**  A measuring seat's scratch evaporates with its session, and nothing in the harness asks it to hand the log back.  **DISPOSITION: the numbers are NOT retracted -- they are a seat's measured report with its method stated and a matched control, which is the ordinary evidentiary basis here -- but they are re-graded from CONFIRMED to MANUAL, because no artifact backs them.**  Re-deriving costs two mutant builds and one probe run each.  *The general fix is a brief line, not a rule: when a measuring seat reports a score, ask it to write the log to `regress/artifacts/<row>/` -- it has the file access, and the ask costs one sentence in the invocation.*  *** IT IS A CLASS WITH TWO MEMBERS, NOT ONE ROW -- censused 2026-08-31, and BOTH were shipped by the same session two days apart, which makes it a habit rather than two accidents. ***  `grep -rn 'row(\".*\", *how, *\"exit1\")' regress/*.py` returns exactly two rows whose ENTIRE oracle is *the host died*:  **`sgi_re_width_probe.py` `other kill`** (this row) and **`sgi_de_width_probe.py` `tail_acc_x`**.  Every other death assertion in the tree carries a text companion.  **The shared defect: at a site where death is the SHARED answer, a death-only oracle cannot see the message change** -- an edit that reworks the diagnostic while leaving `exit(1)` standing passes, measured 35/35 for `other kill` against a matched control at 34/35.  **The two are NOT equally exposed and the difference is worth stating rather than folding in.**  `tail_acc_x` has a sibling (`tail_acc`) that counts the message text, so the PAIR pins identity even though the row does not; `other kill` explicitly discards the transcript its six `ld kill` siblings test for membership, so nothing pins it.  *So this row is the live one and `tail_acc_x` is the latent one* -- and `tail_acc_x` additionally carries a documented expiry, since the round that fixes `sgideprimop` reds it by succeeding.  **One fix shape covers both: the `ok_txt` companion the siblings already carry.**  Doing them together is the class fix and costs one round instead of two.  *Filed, not fixed in-round: this is coverage debt against SHIPPED detectors sitting above the vacuity floor, which the stopping rule files rather than chases.*  *METHOD NOTE, because the check nearly lied to me: the census `grep -rhc ... | paste -sd+ | bc` printed **0** while the listing showed two -- `grep -c` emits nothing for non-matching files, so the sum was taken over an empty set.  The count cited here is from the LISTING, not from that arithmetic.  A denominator that disagrees with a listing you can see is the cheapest signal there is.*"
