   "title": "gen_codex_wall.py's marker regex matches ONE seat name, so a stage held awaiting SEVEN seats is invisible to section I -- the biggest holds are the ones that escape",
   "witness": "harness:MEASURED against HEAD and working ledgers.  `MARKER = re.compile(r'\\(HELD-AWAITING-SEAT/defanged:\\s*([a-z0-9_]+)\\s*\\]')` -- the class has no comma, so `(HELD-AWAITING-SEAT/defanged: opus5, codex, grok, kimi, glm, deepseek, minimax)` does not match.  Enumerated: 2 multi-seat markers present, of which `sgirekills` is a genuine live hold owing all seven scriptable seats and is counted NOWHERE.  CLOSED by 9abd175: a strict token grammar plus seat-id validation against the ledger's own roster, controlled in both directions -- real ids render clean with empty stderr, an unknown id renders `?token` and names row, phase and token, and the real ledger is unchanged at 8 held rows with no warnings.  Controls preserved at regress/artifacts/holdmarkermulti/controls_20260830/controls.out.",
   "note": "*** A CHECK THAT CANNOT FIRE ON ITS LARGEST CASES. ***  `gen_codex_wall.py` exists because section I of `precommit_check.sh` requires that every `(HELD-AWAITING-SEAT/defanged: ...)` marker is ALSO NAMED IN THE QUEUE -- its own docstring: *holding a stage and queueing it are ONE action, not two*.  **Its regex accepts a single `[a-z0-9_]+` seat name.**  A marker naming two or more seats contains commas, fails the match, and is therefore neither counted as a held stage nor required to appear in the queue.  **So the enforcement is strongest on the SMALLEST holds and absent on the biggest.**  A stage awaiting one seat is policed; a stage awaiting all seven is not -- and a seven-seat hold is, by construction, the one carrying the most owed work.  *** MEASURED, not argued: `sgirekills` is held awaiting `opus5, codex, grok, kimi, glm, deepseek, minimax` and `gen_codex_wall.py` reports it in no count. ***  **THE FIX IS THE CHARACTER CLASS** -- admit `[a-z0-9_,\\s]+` and split on commas.  Note the class must stay RESTRICTED: `ledgerstatelies` QUOTES the literal inside prose containing `*` and `-`, and a permissive `[^\\]]+` would match that quotation and manufacture a phantom hold -- **the quoted-marker hazard the carrier already records, arriving from the other direction.**  That prose is the built-in negative control.  **OWED CONTROLS (a tooling change owes them both ways):** (1) POSITIVE -- `sgirekills` appears as held after the widening; (2) NEGATIVE -- `ledgerstatelies`' quoted prose still does NOT; (3) NEGATIVE -- a defanged marker still does not.  *Found while defanging stale markers: the count did not move for the multi-seat ones, and a count that does not move is not thereby correct -- which is the same tell the carrier records from the stuck 27.*  *** AND I QUOTED THE LITERAL IN THIS VERY ROW WHILE DOCUMENTING THE HAZARD OF QUOTING IT -- two live copies, in `witness` and `note`, defanged the same day. ***  They were harmless TODAY because `held_rows()` reads only `entries[].note`; they would fire the moment any tool widens its scan.  *The carrier's rule is: before quoting a string a tool matches on, ask what that tool will do with your copy.  I wrote the row that says so and then did not ask.*  **A SECOND, SMALLER FINDING FROM THE SAME COUNT:** `gen_codex_wall.py` prints `N held stages` and `held_rows()` returns ONE TUPLE PER ROW, phases joined -- so the number is ROWS, not stages.  Today that is 8 rows carrying 9 (row, phase) stages, because `wdccdbmidxfer` is held at both `research` and `regress`.  **STAGES AND ROWS ARE DIFFERENT UNITS**, which this project has already paid for once when four counting methods gave four answers to one question.  The label is wrong, not the tool.  *** TWO CLAIMS IN THIS ROW WERE REFUTED BY THE PANEL AND ARE CORRECTED HERE RATHER THAN SILENTLY SUPERSEDED. ***  (1) It prescribed admitting `[a-z0-9_,\\s]` -- MEASURED to span NEWLINES and replaced by a strict token grammar `[a-z0-9_]+(?:[ \t]*,[ \t]*[a-z0-9_]+)*`, which also kills the `split(\", \")` separator mutant a seat found surviving the character class.  (2) It called the `ledgerstatelies` quotation a 'built-in negative control'.  It is VACUOUS: that row has ZERO entries and its quotation sits in a row-level field `held_rows()` never reads, so the permissive class this row forbade passes every control and renders byte-identically.  Both found pre-ship (L1).  *A row filed to document a check-that-cannot-fail shipped with a check that could not fail.*",
